**Recommended role setup for safe SharePoint access**

The two organization-level roles serve distinct purposes — and both shape SharePoint exposure:

* **Org owner — complete control.** The only role that governs the SharePoint integration: which sites Normain can access at all, and which sites are connected to which teams. Whoever holds Org owner defines Normain's entire SharePoint data surface.

* **Org manager — user and team administration.** Manages who belongs to which team, team billing/usage statistics, and general org administration. Using this role is **optional -** Org owners can do everything an Org manager can, so KPMG may run with Org owners alone. An Org manager cannot configure the SharePoint integration — but, importantly, an Org manager can **add itself to any team**, and a team member can see the SharePoint sites connected to that team. An Org manager can therefore reach **every SharePoint site connected to any team** — in effect, the union of all teams' SharePoint access.

1. **Keep Org owner tightly held** — a small number of trusted KPMG administrators. This role alone wires SharePoint sites to teams, so it defines the whole exposure.
2. **Treat Org manager as a highly trusted role.** Because an Org manager can add itself to any team, it can reach every SharePoint site any team is connected to — effectively all SharePoint data. If you use the role, limit it to a small number of people entitled to that level of access.
3. **Team owner and Team member carry no SharePoint management permissions** — granting these roles does not widen exposure.

Net: SharePoint exposure is set by two levers — site-to-team connections (**Org owner**) and team membership (**Org owner** or **Org manager**, either of whom can place users — including themselves — into any team). Keep both org roles with trusted KPMG administrators; Team owner and Team member can be distributed freely. This fits the one-site-per-engagement model we discussed.

Happy to walk through any of this or adjust the model to your governance requirements.

